---
import LegalPage from '@/components/legal/legal-page.astro'
import LegalSection from '@/components/legal/legal-section.astro'
import { legalUpdated } from '@/lib/legal'
import { externalLinks, legalLinks, support } from '@/lib/links'
---

<LegalPage
  description="Treid's terms of service: the rules for using the hosted Treid with your Alpaca account, your API keys and your workspaces."
  title="Terms of service"
  updated={legalUpdated['/terms']}
>
  <LegalSection title="What Treid does">
    <p>
      Treid is an app that uses your Alpaca API keys to show your Alpaca account and send orders to
      it. <strong>Alpaca is your broker, not Treid.</strong> Alpaca holds your money and positions,
      fills your orders and is bound by its own terms with you. Treid never holds funds, and
      nothing in Treid is investment advice.
    </p>
    <p>
      Treid is infrastructure. It gives you the screens and the server to use what your Alpaca
      account already does: trade stocks, ETFs, options and crypto, manage orders and positions,
      follow market data and keep deposit addresses. On top of that it adds workspaces, so a team
      can trade together on shared connections, with roles and a history of every order and change made through Treid. Treid acts only on the
      instructions you and your workspace members give it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>You need your own Alpaca account to use Treid.</strong> Treid doesn't open one for you.
      A paper account is enough to start, and you can <a href={externalLinks.alpacaSignUp}
        >sign up at Alpaca</a
      >.
    </p>
    <p>By signing up you agree to these terms and to the <a href={legalLinks.privacy}>privacy policy</a>.</p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection title="Signing up">
    <ul>
      <li>You must verify your email before you can sign in with a password.</li>
      <li>Sign-up may be closed, or open only to invited email addresses.</li>
      <li>Keep your password to yourself. What happens in your account is your responsibility.</li>
    </ul>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection title="Your keys and your orders">
    <ul>
      <li>
        Adding keys lets Treid read that Alpaca account and place, change and cancel orders in it
        when you or a workspace member asks.
      </li>
      <li>
        Treid sends each order as entered. Check the symbol, side, quantity, order type and whether
        you're on paper or live before you confirm.
      </li>
      <li>
        Closing a position or canceling all orders acts on every matching item in that Alpaca account,
        including ones placed outside Treid.
      </li>
      <li>
        Prices, charts and news come from Alpaca and can be delayed or wrong. Alpaca's own records are
        the final word on what was filled and at what price.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection title="Keeping your keys safe">
    <p>
      <strong>Your Alpaca keys are your responsibility.</strong> A key ID and secret can do whatever
      Alpaca allows for that account, live trading included. Treid encrypts the keys it stores, but it
      can't control keys that are copied, shared or leaked anywhere else.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Treat the secret like a password. Paste it only into a Treid connection you control.</li>
      <li>Never send keys in chat, email, screenshots or code, and never commit them to a repository.</li>
      <li>
        Add live keys to a workspace only when you trust every member whose role permits live trading.
        Those members can place orders with that account through Treid.
      </li>
      <li>Use paper keys while you learn the app or try a new workspace.</li>
      <li>
        If you think a key was exposed, revoke it in the Alpaca dashboard right away and create a new
        one. Treid can't revoke Alpaca keys for you.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Losses from keys you shared, leaked or let others use are yours, not Treid's. The same applies
      when you run your own copy of Treid.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection title="Workspaces">
    <ul>
      <li>Keys added to a workspace belong to that workspace and stay when the person who added them leaves.</li>
      <li>
        Anyone whose role allows trading can place orders with the workspace's connections, live ones
        included. Owners and admins choose those roles and answer for who they invite.
      </li>
      <li>Every order and change is logged with the name of the member who made it.</li>
      <li>A workspace always keeps at least one owner.</li>
    </ul>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection title="Crypto deposit addresses">
    <p>
      Treid shows the deposit addresses exactly as you saved them. Crypto sent to a wrong address
      or on the wrong network usually can't be recovered. Check the address and the network against
      Alpaca before you send anything.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection title="Things you can't do">
    <ul>
      <li>Use keys or an Alpaca account you aren't allowed to use.</li>
      <li>Use Treid to break the law, Alpaca's terms or market rules.</li>
      <li>Try to get into other people's accounts or workspaces, or overload the service.</li>
    </ul>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection title="Open source">
    <p>
      The code is <a href={externalLinks.github}>public on GitHub</a>. These terms cover the hosted
      service on this site only. If you run your own copy, you run it on your own terms.
    </p>
    <p>
      Self-hosting moves the responsibility to whoever runs the server. That person keeps the
      database, the encryption keys and the Alpaca keys safe, decides who can sign in, and keeps the
      copy up to date. Everyone who uses a self-hosted copy still answers for their own keys.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection title="No guarantees">
    <p>
      Treid is provided as is. It can have bugs and outages, and Alpaca can too. As far as the law
      allows, we aren't liable for trading losses, orders that didn't go through, orders sent as
      entered, or losses from delayed or wrong data.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection title="Ending your use">
    <p>
      You can stop at any time. Remove your connections, or revoke the keys in Alpaca. To delete your
      account, email <a href={support.href}>{support.email}</a>. We may suspend an account that
      breaks these terms or puts other users at risk.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection title="Changes">
    <p>
      When these terms change, the date at the top changes too. We'll email you before a change
      takes effect.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>
</LegalPage>
